00:00:24.585 --> 00:00:25.973
Hi, hello.

00:00:26.275 --> 00:00:32.720
It’s me again, Deboki, writer and occasional
host for Journey to the Microcosmos.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:40.220
And I am here to confess something kind of
shameful to you all: the first time I saw

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:45.650
a picture of a tardigrade, I didn’t think
it was real.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:51.690
I just remember looking at this animal that
looked like a cross between a dog and the

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.560
Michelin Man.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:58.140
And it was even more strange to conceive of
how tiny it is, and that out there in the

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:03.150
world are just bits of moss with animals like
this one gliding through them.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:09.270
Now that I’ve spent some more time in the
microcosmos, tardigrades seem almost normal.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:10.270
Almost.

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:16.210
I mean, they are animals, and at the end of
the day, you don’t have to be tiny to be

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.579
a weird-looking animal.

00:01:18.579 --> 00:01:25.130
But animals have the advantage of many, many
cells to build their different bodies.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:32.250
On the other hand, unicellular organisms like
bacteria and protists, have less to work with.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:37.520
And yet they manage to take on so many strange
and unusual forms.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.240
Just think about it: if you were asked to
draw a cell, what would you draw?

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.990
Maybe your imagination is better than mine,
but I would probably sketch out a vaguely

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:52.549
round thing, maybe add some extra hairs on
the outside if I’m feeling creative.

00:01:52.549 --> 00:01:56.259
Nature has a lot more clever ideas though.

00:01:56.259 --> 00:02:02.520
Trumpets and spirals and floral arrangements,
all made out of single cells.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.780
Of course, these shapes aren’t just for
fun.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:11.210
An organism’s shape is connected to the
world it lives in, though sometimes the factors

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:13.500
shaping them are beyond our understanding.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:20.810
It can be difficult at times to narrow down
why a microbe takes on a particular shape

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:27.390
when the reality of the microcosmos is that
in any environment, you can find a whole plethora

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:30.660
of shapes that might seem to have nothing
in common.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:36.890
It’s also easy to look at the existence
of a living thing and assume that every detail

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:43.560
of its being has been optimized through eons
of mutations and selection for a specific

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:44.560
purpose.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.750
And there is an extent to which that’s true.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:54.190
The world places all sorts of demands on life:
the need for food, for reproduction, for warmth,

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:56.600
for light, for dark, for movement.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:02.920
And we respond in kind by prioritizing and
balancing those needs, and shape is one way

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:06.140
we do that.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:11.200
But it’s one thing to come up with stories
for why something might be advantageous, It’s

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.190
a much more involved process to prove it.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:19.900
Take spirochetes, for example, which are distinctive
thanks to their helical shape.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.690
They’re also…not great for us.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:27.580
They’re infectious, able to dig into us
and invade our bodies.

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:32.090
And it’s been hypothesized that part of
how they do this is through that corkscrew

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:38.910
shape, which might help them move through
thick viscous solutions, you know, like our insides.

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:42.490
But proving this hypothesis took a lot of
work.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:47.210
Scientists spent decades studying spirochetes
in viscous solutions and mouse stomachs, as

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:52.570
well as comparing them to other bacteria,
all just to show that this shape gives them

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:55.730
a physical advantage in colonizing bodies.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:04:00.820
And even then there are still so many aspects
of the helical life and the advantages it

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:03.820
provides them that are still conjecture.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:11.340
And that’s true for bacteria in general,
which can take on so many shapes: spheres,

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:13.720
rods, filaments, stalks.

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:19.310
But whatever the shape, the building material
is the same: peptidoglycan, a combination

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:25.199
of amino acid and sugar that links together
to form a strong lattice that holds the bacteria

00:04:25.199 --> 00:04:27.719
and all its contents within.

00:04:27.719 --> 00:04:32.750
The structure of the bacterial cell wall can
vary along with its shape.

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:38.039
One group of bacteria, called gram-negative
bacteria, generally have thinner walls and

00:04:38.039 --> 00:04:40.729
an additional outer membrane.

00:04:40.729 --> 00:04:45.389
Gram-positive bacteria, in contrast, generally
have a thicker, multi-layered wall.

00:04:45.389 --> 00:04:53.370
But like many categorizations in biology,
this dichotomy is just a tad bit too simple.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:05:00.310
One of the largest groups of bacteria are
cyanobacteria, and their diversity is even

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:01.979
more astounding.

00:05:01.979 --> 00:05:07.620
There are cyanobacteria like the Merismopedia,
for example, that form flat colonies held

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:15.439
together by a mucilaginous matrix like this,
their round shape neatly arranged like candy dots.

00:05:15.439 --> 00:05:22.900
And then you have other cyanobacteria like
these long filamentous forms that glide along surfaces.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:27.600
Cyanobacteria are generally categorized as
gram-negative bacteria because their cells

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.139
have that extra outer membrane we talked about.

00:05:31.139 --> 00:05:38.241
But cyanobacteria also have a thick peptidoglycan
layer, which makes them sort of like gram-positive

00:05:38.241 --> 00:05:40.139
bacteria too.

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:46.879
And that strange combination of traits underlies
the spectacular diversity of cyanobacteria.

00:05:46.879 --> 00:05:52.710
Single-celled eukaryotes, or protists, have
a wide range of exteriors too.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:57.620
But much of our understanding of their shape
comes from their cytoskeleton, a network of

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:03.979
filaments and microtubules that coordinates
many essential functions in the cell.

00:06:03.979 --> 00:06:09.139
And when it comes to shape, it might seem
unusual to focus on the amoeba, which is so

00:06:09.139 --> 00:06:13.439
often known for its shapelessness, especially
as they move.

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:20.539
It’s hard not to look at a blob and think
of it as accidental, but the forming of pseudopodia--or

00:06:20.539 --> 00:06:26.279
false feet--to help the amoeba move, requires
a complex architecture of microtubules to

00:06:26.279 --> 00:06:29.150
support this shifting shape.

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:33.229
And of course, there are many protists that
take on very different shapes, whether

00:06:33.229 --> 00:06:37.199
that’s for defense or for food or for some
other reason.

00:06:37.199 --> 00:06:44.469
There are pediastrum, that form these spiky
colonies that protect them from algae eaters.

00:06:44.469 --> 00:06:50.020
And then there are the Opercularia, which
look like little flowers attached to branches,

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:56.509
and whose cilia you can see at work gathering
food as it floats on by.

00:06:56.509 --> 00:07:04.110
It’d be difficult to describe all the shapes
that protists take on because there are just

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:09.550
so many, even--it feels like--when compared
to bacteria.

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:15.379
Some scientists have argued that bacterial
diversity is more internal than external,

00:07:15.379 --> 00:07:19.089
a function of metabolic reactions we can’t
see.

00:07:19.089 --> 00:07:24.509
But protists have adapted more with their
shape, leaving us with a world where the diversity

00:07:24.509 --> 00:07:26.709
itself is multifaceted.

00:07:31.651 --> 00:07:36.751
We’ve spent this episode talking about the
diversity of shapes in the microcosmos, in

00:07:36.751 --> 00:07:38.900
part because that’s what we can show you.

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:44.740
But we’ve seen over and over again the way
that shapes have misled scientists when it

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:49.770
comes to classifying organisms, or the way
that the shape of something can actually lead

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:55.560
to more questions that can only be answered
by studying the inside of the cell.

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:03.080
But that’s the joy of shape: it’s an entrypoint
into the microcosmos, a way for us to wonder

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:14.509
about organism and environment alike.

00:08:14.509 --> 00:08:19.909
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

